A||||4||CHUCKVH||KYB shock problem?||||1||
Z||000000||CHUCKVH||02-14-2001||12:21 AM||||Last week I replaced the lower ball joints and front shocks on my 88.  When I compared the original shocks to the new KYB's, I saw that the KYB gas shocks are approximately 1-1/4 to 1-1/2 inches longer in both compression and extension.  This means that my suspension drops farther (upper A-arm contacts spring tower) and the shock may "bottom out" before the suspension travel is stopped by the lower A-arm rubber bumper.  I double checked the part numbers, application, etc, and all seems correct.  Has anybody had any trouble with this?  The only solution that I can see is to use steel spacers and longer bolts at the lower shock mount, but that reduces ground clearance, etc.<p>[This message has been edited by CHUCKVH (edited 02-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||CHUCKVH||02-16-2001||01:06 AM||||Its been a couple of days and no comments?  Hasn't anyone ever noticed this shock absorber dimension problem before?||||reg||1
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||02-16-2001||08:35 AM||||nope, mine lined right up, I don't have the part number, but I have to put the fiero on the hoist tonight for the altinator, I'll see what I can find.||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||02-16-2001||11:36 AM|||| in any given month here at the shop we usually replace the shocks and struts in about 5 or 6 Fieros. I have experienced no problem with these parts as far as being the wrong dimensions - the only thins I can suggest is giving me the part number to se if they are correct, or - simply put them on your car and road test to see if they will be problematic<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000004||CHUCKVH||02-16-2001||11:20 PM||||Okay, remember that this is an 88:<P>The catalog and the KYB website list the front shocks as P/N KG-4513.<P>Measuring the shocks from the bottom end mounting surface to the top of the lower rubber "bumper", the original shocks were 12-5/8" at full extension, and 8-7/8" at full compression.  The KYB's are 13-3/4" at full extension, and 9-1/4" at full compression.  My only concern is that the original shocks wear pattern indicated that they were going to almost full compression, and if that happens with my new shocks, they will bottom out and cause some damage before the suspension completes its full travel.<P>I have had them installed for a couple of weeks now with no problems, but then again I have been commuting on smooth roads with no big dips, speed bumps, or pot holes.||||reg||1
